    "ls|LS17_S11_U_141": " \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CULTURE \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION  NO. †*141 \nANSWERED ON 13.02.2023 \n \n \nRESTORATION AND DEVELOPMENT OF MONUMENTS \n \n Will the Minister of CULTURE be pleased to state: \n \n \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF CULTURE, TOURISM AND DEVELOPMENT OF NORTH EASTERN \nREGION \n(SHRI G. KISHAN REDDY) \n \n \n(a) to (e) A statement is laid on the table of the House. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n†*141. SHRI DINESH LAL YADAV “NIRAHUA”: \nSHRI C.R. PATIL: \n(a) whether many archaeological monuments located in various States of the country need to \nbe restored and developed; \n(b) \n \nif so, the steps taken/being taken by the Government to restore and develop these \nmonuments along with the details of the schemes to provide funds to State \nArchaeological Departments; \n(c) whether several monuments related to sages in Azamgarh, Uttar Pradesh are being \nproposed to be restored and developed along with the ASI protected sites in Gujarat; \n(d) if so, the time by which it is likely to be done; and \n(e) if not, the reason therefor? \n\n \n \n \n---- \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n                                                                                                                             \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) TO (e) OF THE LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. †*141 FOR 13.02.2023. \n \n(a) \n  & \n  (b) \n   \n \n \n \nArchaeological Survey of India (ASI) has 3696 Centrally Protected Monuments \nin various States of the country. ASI is taking up conservation work of \nmonuments as and when necessary, as per availability of resources.  Upkeep of \nthe monuments is a continuous process.  There is no scheme to provide funds to \nthe State Archaeology Departments by the Government of India. \n      (c) \n       to \n      (e) \nThere are no such monuments protected by ASI in Azamgarh, Uttar Pradesh.  ASI \nhas 205 centrally protected monuments/sites in the State of Gujarat. Conservation \nworks are carried out as per requirement and they are in a good state of \npreservation. \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S11_U_142": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 142 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 13.02.2023 \n \nSUICIDES BY DAILY WAGERS \n \n*142. SHRI THIRUNAVUKKARASAR SU: \n  \nWill the Minister of LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a)whether it is a fact that daily wagers are the largest group among \nthose who have died by suicide during the last few years; \n(b)if so, the details thereof and the number of cases reported in the \ncountry during the last three years, year-wise; \n(c)whether any steps  are being taken by the Government to protect \nthe daily wage earners from suicides and to improve their \nlivelihood; \n(d)if so, the details thereof; and \n(e)if not, the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n(SHRI BHUPENDER YADAV) \n \n \n(a) to (e): A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n**                                                   ****** \n  \n\nSTATEMENT  REFERRED TO REPLY TO PART (a) & ( e ) OF LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 142* FOR 13.02.2023 REGARDING “SUICIDES \nBY DAILY WAGERS” RAISED BY SHRI THIRUNAVUKKARASAR SU. \n \n(a) & (b): The profession -wise number of suicides committed by \npeople in the country during 2019 to 2021 is given at Annexure as per \nlatest published data till year 2021 , received from National Crime \nRecords Bureau (NCRB). \n \n(c) &(d): As per the Unorganised Workers‟ Social Security Act, 2008, \nthe Government is mandated to provide Social Security to the workers \nof unorganised sector including daily wage workers by formulating \nsuitable welfare schemes on matters relatin g to (i) life and disability \ncover, (ii) health and maternity benefits, (iii) old age protection and (iv) \nany other benefit as may be determined by the Central Government. \n \nThe Life and Disability cover is provided through Pradhan \nMantri Jeevan Jyoti Bima Yojana (PMJJBY) and Pradhan Mantri \nSuraksha Bima Yojana (PMSBY).  PMJJBY is available to the people in \nthe age group of 18 to 50 years having a bank/post office account who \ngive their consent to join/enable auto debit. Risk coverage under this \nscheme is fo r Rs. 2.00 lakh in case of death of insured, due to any \nreason, at an annual premium of Rs. 436/ - which is to be auto debited \nfrom the subscriber‟s bank/post office account. As on 31.12.2022, a \ntotal of 14.82 crore beneficiaries have been enrolled under th e \nscheme. \nThe Pradhan Mantri Suraksha Bima Yojana (PMSBY) is also \navailable to the people in the age group of 18 to 70 years with a \nbank/post office account who give their consent to join/enable auto \ndebit. The risk coverage under the scheme is for Rs 2.00  lakh in case \nof accidental death or total permanent disability and Rs. 1.00 lakh for \npartial permanent disability; due to accident at a premium of Rs. 20 \nper annum which is to be deducted from the account holder‟s \nbank/post office account through „auto -debit‟.  As on 31.12.2022, a \ntotal of 31.88 crore beneficiaries have been enrolled under the \nscheme. \nThe Ayushman Bharat -Pradhan Mantri Jan Arogya Yojana \n(ABPMJAY) provides an annual health cover of Rs . 5 lakhs per eligible \nfamily for secondary and tertiary care hospitalization corresponding to \n1949 treatment procedures across 27 specialties. As on 4th \nDecember, 2022, a total of 20.96 crore beneficiaries have been \nprovided with the Ayushman Cards. \n\n \nIn o rder to provide old age protection, the Government of \nIndia launched Pradhan Mantri Shram Yogi Maan -Dhan (PM -SYM) \npension scheme in 2019. It provides monthly pension of Rs.3000/ - after \nattaining the age of 60 years. The workers in the age group of 18 -40 \nyears whose monthly income is Rs. 15000/ - or less and not a member \nof EPFO/ESIC/NPS (Govt. funded) can join the PM -SYM Scheme. Under \nthis scheme 50% monthly contribution is payable by the beneficiary \nand equal matching contribution is paid by the Central Government. As \non 30.01.2023, a total of 49.25 lakh beneficiaries have been enrolled \nunder the scheme. \n \nApart from above, other schemes such as Public \nDistribution System through One Nation One Ration Card scheme \nunder National Food Security Act, Mahatma Gand hi National Rural \nEmployment Guarantee Act, Deen Dayal Upadhyay Gramin Kaushal \nYojana, Pradhan Mantri Awas Yojana, Gareeb Kalyan Rojgar Abhiyan, \nMahatma Gandhi Bunkar Bima Yojana, Deen Dayal Antyodaya Yojana, \nPMSVANidhi, Pradhan Mantri Kaushal Vikas Yojana  etc. are also \navailable for the unorganised workers including labourers depending \nupon their eligibility criteria. \n \ne-SHRAM portal was launched in August, 2021 with an \nobjective to create National Database of Unorganised Workers (NDUW) \nof the age group of  16-59 years to facilitate delivery of Social Security \nSchemes/Welfare Schemes of the Central and State Governments to \nthe unorganized workers. \n \n(e):  Does not arise. \n  \n\nANNEXURE \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO REPLY TO PART (a ) & (b) OF LOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION \nNO. 142* FOR 13.02.2023 REGARDING “SUICIDES BY DAILY WAGERS” RAISED BY SHRI \nTHIRUNAVUKKARASAR SU. \n \nProfession-wise Number of Suicides committed by people in the country during 2019-2021 \nSl.No Profession 2019 2020 2021 \n1 Housewife 21359 22374 23179 \n2 Professional/Salaried Persons (Total) 12725 14825 15870 \n2.1 Govt. Servants (Total) 1684 2057 1898 \n2.1.1 Central/UT Govt. Servants 307 439 381 \n2.1.2 State Govt. Servants 968 1089 1131 \n2.1.3 Other Statutory Body/etc. 409 529 386 \n2.2 Private Sector Enterprises 8730 10166 11431 \n2.3 Public Sector Undertaking 2311 2602 2541 \n3 Students 10335 12526 13089 \n4 Unemployed Persons 14019 15652 13714 \n5 Self-employed Persons (Total) 16098 17332 20231 \n5.1 Business (Total) 9052 11716 12055 \n5.1.1 Vendor 3351 4226 4532 \n5.1.2 Tradesmen 2906 4356 3633 \n5.1.3 Others 2795 3134 3890 \n5.2 Other Self-employed Persons 7046 5616 8176 \n6 Persons engaged in Farming Sector (Total) 10281 10677 10881 \n6.1 Farmers/Cultivators  5957 5579 5318 \n6.2 Agricultural Labourers 4324 5098 5563 \n7 Daily Wage Earner 32563 37666 42004 \n8 Retired Persons 1302 1457 1518 \n9 Other Persons 20441 20543 23547 \n Total 139123 153052 164033 \n \n***** ",
    "ls|LS17_S11_U_143": " \nPosition no. 03 \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FINANCE \nDEPARTMENT OF ECONOMIC AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *143 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON MONDAY 13th FEBRUARY, 2023 \n[Magha 24, 1944 (SAKA)] \n \n‘Support to States for their Capital Expenditure’ \n \nNo. *143.  Shri  A. Ganeshamurthi \n      Shri A. Raja  \n  \nWill the MINISTER OF FINANCE be pleased to state: \n  \n(a) whether the Government has released instalments of tax due to every State from \ndivisible pool to help accelerate their capital expenditure and if so, the details \nthereof including the amount of instalment of tax released to the States out of the \ndivisible pool during each of the last five years, State/ UT-wise;  \n \n(b) the details and the amount of provisional GST compensation yet to be released \nfor the financial year 2021-22, State/UT-wise including Tamil Nadu;  \n \n(c) whether there has been a delay in the release of the said provisional GST \ncompensation to the States, which are reeling under financial stress and if so, the \ndetails thereof and the reasons therefor; and  \n \n(d)     the time by which all dues will be released to the States? \n \n \nAnswer \n----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- \n \nFINANCE MINISTER     (SMT. NIRMALA SITHARAMAN) \n \n(a) to (d) : A Statement is placed on the Table of the House. \n**** \n \n  \n\n \nStatement referred to in reply to Lok Sabha Starred Question No. * 143 for answer on \nFebruary 13th, 2023 raised by Shri  A. Ganeshamurthi and Shri A. Raja  \n \n(a) : Yes, Sir.  The amount of tax devolved to the States for the last five years is \ndetailed in Annexure.  \n \n(b) to (d)  : Taking into account ₹ 1.10 lakh crore for the financial year 2020 -21 and ₹ 1.59 \nlakh crore for the financial  year 2021 -22 released to States/UTs as back to back \nloan in lieu of GST Compensation and the regular GST Compensation paid to \nStates as per provisi ons of GST (Compensation to States) Act, 2017, entire \nprovisional GST Compensation dues of States/UTs has been cleared by the Union \nGovernment for the financial year 2020-21 and up to 31.05.2022.  \n \n***  \n  \n\nANNEXURE \nAmount Devolved to the States \n                                                                                                                                             (Rs. Crore) \nSl. \nNo \nState 2017-18 2018-19 2019-20 2020-21 2021-22 \n2022-23  \ntill \nFebruary \n1 Andhra Pradesh 29001.25 32787.03 28242.39 24460.59 35385.83 32703.24 \n2 Arunachal Pradesh 9238.79 10436.14 8987.57 10472.58 14643.90 14198.13 \n3 Assam 22301.52 25215.85 21721.44 18629.32 28150.55 25276.96 \n4 Bihar 65083.19 73602.96 63406.33 59861.41 91352.62 81277.42 \n5 Chhattisgarh 20754.83 23458.69 20205.84 20337.54 28570.86 27531.63 \n6 Goa 2544.27 2878.36 2479.85 2296.53 3356.98 3119.17 \n7 Gujarat 20782.34 23489.32 20232.09 20218.53 31105.78 28105.37 \n8 Haryana 7297.53 8254.60 7111.53 6437.59 9722.16 8832.33 \n9   Himachal Pradesh 4801.27 5429.77 4677.56 4753.92 7349.04 6707.11 \n10 Jammu & Kashmir 11911.66 13989.80 6801.81 0.00 0.00 0.00 \n11 Jharkhand 21143.64 23906.13 20593.04 19712.23 27734.64 26723.39 \n12 Karnataka 31751.99 35894.83 30919.00 21694.11 33283.58 29471.09 \n13 Kerala 16833.07 19038.17 16401.05 11560.40 17820.09 15555.75 \n14 Madhya Pradesh 50853.06 57486.87 49517.61 46922.16 69541.50 63434.82 \n15 Maharashtra 37203.32 42050.43 36219.64 36504.01 54318.06 51046.83 \n16 Manipur 4154.29 4698.59 4047.77 4271.97 6009.65 5785.97 \n17 Meghalaya 4323.15 4889.07 4211.78 4551.63 6580.63 6198.08 \n18 Mizoram 3097.04 3502.96 3017.80 3010.55 4222.87 4040.51 \n19 Nagaland 3353.09 3792.41 3267.08 3409.25 4875.46 4597.96 \n20 Odisha 31272.03 35353.73 30453.25 27542.67 38144.79 36590.21 \n21 Punjab 10616.96 12008.95 10345.78 10638.21 15288.79 14602.19 \n22 Rajasthan 37028.03 41852.35 36049.14 35575.77 54030.61 48695.39 \n23 Sikkim 2470.52 2794.67 2407.69 2308.47 3353.69 3135.48 \n24 Tamil Nadu 27099.72 30638.87 26392.40 24924.51 37458.60 32961.87 \n25 Telangana 16420.23 18560.88 15987.59 12691.62 18720.54 16986.06 \n26 Tripura 4322.54 4888.95 4211.78 4218.45 6077.52 5721.31 \n27 Uttar Pradesh 120940.10 136766.46 117818.30 106687.01 160358.05 144962.75 \n28 Uttarakhand 7084.90 8011.59 6901.54 6568.72 9906.25 9034.57 \n29 West Bengal 49321.13 55775.72 48048.40 44737.01 65540.75 60792.47 \n  TOTAL 673005.46 761454.15 650677.05 594996.76 882903.79 808088.06 \n \n*** \n  ",
    "ls|LS17_S11_U_144": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EDUCATION \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 144 \nANSWERED ON 13.02.2023 \n \n \nProject to Rewrite Indian History \n \n*144. SHRI SUBBARAYAN K.: \n \nWill the Minister of EDUCATION be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Indian Council of Historical Research (ICHR) has launched a project to \nrewrite Indian history; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof and the reasons therefor; and \n \n(c) the details of the main historians involved in this project, the time by which the  said \nproject is likely to be completed and the expected total cost involved therein to complete the \nproject? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF EDUCATION \n \n(SHRI DHARMENDRA PRADHAN) \n \n(a) to (c): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House.    \n \n \n***** \n \n  \n\n \n \nStatement referred to in reply to parts (a) to (c) in respect of Lok Sabha Starred \nQuestion No. *144 for reply on 13.02.2023 regarding “Project to Rewrite Indian \nHistory” asked by SHRI SUBBARAYAN K., Hon’ble Member of Parliament. \n \n(a) to (c): No, Sir. The Indian Council of Historical Research (ICHR), Delhi has not launched \nany project to rewrite Indian history.    \n \n*** \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S11_U_145": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 145 \nANSWERED ON 02nd FEBRUARY, 2023 \n \nROAD PROJECTS IN HARYANA \n145.  SHRI DHARAMBIR SINGH: \n \nWill the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \nसड़क परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \n \nbe pleased to state: \n(a) the details of road projects being implemented by the Government \nin the state of Haryana district-wise including Bhiwani-Mahendragarh \nalong with the cost thereof; \n(b)   the details of proposals received by the Union Government from \nthe Government of Haryana during the last five years and the current \nyears; and \n(c)   the present status of the said proposals? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \n(SHRI NITIN JAIRAM GADKARI) \n(a)   Total 47 projects amounting to Rs. 36664 crore  are under \nimplementation in the state of Haryana  including in the districts of  \nBhiwani and Mahendragarh . Project details are not maintained \ndistrict-wise but are maintained state-wise. \n(b)& (c)      Total 22  proposals amounting to Rs. 3743 crore have been \nreceived during the last 5 year s and the current year. Out of these, \nprojects amounting to Rs. 46 3 crore have been completed , projects \namounting to Rs. 1530 crore are under implementation  and the \nprojects amounting to Rs. 1671 crore are in the bidding stage. \n***** ",
    "ls|LS17_S11_U_146": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 146\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON\n  \n02.02.2023\n \n \nPUBLIC PROCUREMENT POLICY\n \n \n146. \n \n \nSHRI RAVNEET SINGH BITTU\n:\n \n \nWill the Minister of MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES be pleased to state:\n \n \n(a) \n \nthe\n \ndetails of the Micro and Small Enterprises (MSEs) share in the procurement of goods \nand services through Central Public Sector Enterprises (CPSEs) during the last three years, year\n-\nwise;\n \n(b) \n \nwhether the Government is taking any steps to encourage the CPS\nEs towards fulfilling the \nmandate of Public Procurement Policy;\n \n(\nc) \n \nif so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor;\n \n(d) \n \nwhether the Government has taken any steps to encourage support by the CPSEs towards \nSC/ST and women entrepreneurs in the\n \ncountry; and\n \n(e) \n \nif so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n(SHRI BHANU PRATAP SINGH VERMA)\n \n \n \n(a) \n \nThe details of the Micro and Small Enterprises (MSEs) share in the procurement of goods \nand services through Central Public Sector Enterprises (CPSEs) during the last three years, year\n-\nwise are given below:\n \n \nF.Y\n \nBuyers\n \nTotal \nProcurement \n \n(Rs in \nCrores)\n \nPro\ncurement from \nMSEs(including \nSC/ST owned MSEs)\n \n(Rs in Crores)\n \nProcurement from \nMSEs owned by \nSC/ST\n \n(Rs in Crores)\n \nProcurement from \nMSEs owned by \nWomen\n \n(Rs in Crores)\n \n2019\n-\n20\n \n \n152\n \nCPSEs\n \n131,460.68\n \n \n39,049.45\n \n(29.70%)\n \n(No. of MSEs \nbenefited  1,57,770)\n \n691.43\n \n(0.53%)\n \n(No. of MSEs \nbenefited  6,341)\n \n393.51\n \n(0.30%)\n \n(No. of MSEs \nbenefited  3,666)\n \n2020\n-\n21\n \n \n161\n \nCPSEs\n \n139,419.74\n \n \n40,717.81\n \n(29.21%)\n \n(No. of MSEs \nbenefited  1,77,594)\n \n768.53\n \n(0.55%)\n \n(No. of MSEs \nbenefited  6,870)\n \n749.20\n \n(0.54%)\n \n(No. of MSEs \nbenefited  5,140)\n \n2021\n-\n22\n \n158\n \nCPSEs\n \n164,512.84\n \n \n53,483.02\n \n(32.51%)\n \n(No. of MSEs \nbenefited  2,25,441)\n \n1,290.87\n \n(0.78%)\n \n(No. of MSEs \nbenefited  10,417)\n \n1,660.43\n \n(1.01%)\n \n(No. of MSEs \nbenefited  11,229)\n \n2022\n-\n23 \n(As on \n30.01.20\n23)\n \n135\n \nCPSEs\n \n132,947.37\n \n \n45,094.01\n \n(33.92%)\n \n(No. of MSEs \nbenefited  1,70,790)\n \n1,130.69\n \n(0.85%)\n \n(No. of MSEs \nbenefited  7,512)\n \n1,316.19\n \n(0.99%)\n \n(No. of MSEs \nbenefited  8,928)\n \n(Source MSME SAMBANDH Portal)\n \n \n\n \n: 2 :\n \n \n \n \n(b) & (c):\n \nTo encourage CPSEs to fulfil the mandate of Public \nProcurement Policy, the \nfollowing recent measures have been taken by the Government:\n-\n \n \ni. \n \nVarious conclaves have been organized by the Ministry of MSME and Department of Public \nEnterprises to understand issues and challenges in achieving the targets of pr\nocurement from \nMSEs.\n \n \nii. \n \nThe Review Committee headed by Secretary, MSME regularly monitors the progress made \nin implementation of the Public Procurement Policy. \n \n \niii. Various Vendor Development Programmes have been organised by field offices of \nDevelopm\nent Commissioner (MSME), NSIC and CPSEs to identify vendors for supply of \ngoods and services to CPSEs / Government Departments.\n \n \n(d) & (e): \n \nThe Government has taken various steps to encourage support by the CPSEs towards \nSC/ST and women entrepreneurs in t\nhe country including the launch of SC/ST hub to provide \nhandholding support to SC/ST entrepreneurs, introduction of different category filters on the GeM \nportal, etc. Besides, some other steps have also been taken. These include introduction of online \nregi\nstration\n-\nUdyam Registration for Ease of Doing business, constitution of Review Committee, \nopening a grievance cell to redress the grievances of MSEs, conducting buyer seller meets/ vendor \ndevelopment programmes, reduction of compliance burden, launch of MS\nME Sambandh portal, \netc. \n \n \n \n*****\n \n \n \n \n \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S11_U_147": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 147 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 13.02.2023 \n \nWORKERS REGISTERED IN E-SHRAM PORTAL \n \n†*147. SHRI RATTAN LAL KATARIA: \n           SHRI JAYANT SINHA: \n \n   Will the Minister of LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a)the number of workers in the unorganized sector registered in the e -\nShram portal till 2022, State-wise incuding Jharkhand; \n(b)the type of social security being provided to the workers link ed to \nthe e-Shram portal by the Union Government; \n(c)whether the workers registered in the e -Shram portal are being \nprovided sufficient employment during Covid pandemic, if so, the \ndetails thereof; \n(d)the initiative of the Government to utilise the e -Shram portal for the \ndelivery of schemes to unorganised workers; and \n(e)the steps taken by the Government to ensure maximum registration \non e-Shram in all villages and districts of the country? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n(SHRI BHUPENDER YADAV) \n \n \n(a) to (e): A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n**                                                   ****** \n  \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED  TO IN REPLY  TO PART (a) TO (e)  OF LOK \nSABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. * 147 TO BE ANSWERED ON \n13.02.2023 BY SHRI RATTAN LAL KATARIA  & SHRI JAYANT SINHA \nREGARDING WORKERS REGISTERED IN E-SHRAM PORTAL. \n \n(a): State-wise count of registrations including Jharkhand on e -\nSHRAM portal till 31st December, 2022 is annexed.  \n \n(b) &(c): e-SHRAM portal was launched on 26th Aug ust, 2021, which \nwas after the COVID pandemic subsided. e-SHRAM has been \nintegrated with Pradhan Mantri Shram Yogi Maan -dhan (PM -SYM) \nscheme through which any unorganised worker can register to enrol \nfor pension benefits under the scheme. e-SHRAM portal has also been \nintegrated with National Career Services (NCS) to provide job \nopportunities to unorganised workers.  The workers can apply for \nvarious available job openings/ opportunities on NCS as per their skills, \neducation and experience. \n \n(d): Ministry has dev eloped Standard Operating Procedure (SOP) to \nshare e-SHRAM data with States/ UTs. Based on this SOP, data sharing \nguidelines have been shared with all the States & UTs. Pilot \nintegration for Data Sharing with Haryana has been completed. \nSOP/Guidelines to s hare e -SHRAM data with Central \nMinistries/Departments is also under finalization. The e -SHRAM data \nmay be used by the respective States/ UTs/ Central Ministries for \ndelivery of schemes to the unorganised workers. \n \n(e): e-SHRAM portal can be accessed throug h url: \nhttps://eShram.gov.in/. Ministry has onboarded services of Common \nService Centres (CSC) and State Sewa Kendras (SSKs) to facilitate \nworkers in the registration process. e-SHRAM has also been integrated \nwith UMANG (Unified Mobile Application for New -age Governance) \nmobile application, through which workers can register on e -SHRAM, \nupdate their details and download Universal Account Number (UAN) \ncard using their mobiles. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nANNEXURE \n \nAnnexure referred to in  reply to part (a) of the Lok Sabha  Starred Question No. *147 for \n13.02.2023. \n \nState-wise count of registrations including Jharkhand on eShram portal till 31st December 2022 \nis as under: \nSl.No State Total Registrations \n1 UTTAR PRADESH 8,29,88,073 \n2 BIHAR 2,85,31,705 \n3 WEST BENGAL 2,57,42,622 \n4 MADHYA PRADESH 1,68,25,175 \n5 MAHARASHTRA 1,33,84,893 \n6 ODISHA 1,33,20,014 \n7 RAJASTHAN 1,27,48,263 \n8 JHARKHAND 91,19,684 \n9 GUJARAT 91,07,473 \n10 TAMIL NADU 83,11,915 \n11 CHHATTISGARH 82,42,237 \n12 ANDHRA PRADESH 78,77,639 \n13 KARNATAKA 73,13,170 \n14 ASSAM 68,92,991 \n15 KERALA 59,02,220 \n16 PUNJAB 54,93,649 \n17 HARYANA 52,44,430 \n18 TELANGANA 40,28,938 \n19 JAMMU AND KASHMIR 33,54,810 \n20 DELHI 32,48,331 \n21 UTTARAKHAND 29,70,503 \n22 HIMACHAL PRADESH 19,21,821 \n23 TRIPURA 8,43,374 \n24 MANIPUR 4,04,085 \n25 MEGHALAYA 2,83,531 \n26 NAGALAND 2,18,409 \n27 PUDUCHERRY 1,76,253 \n28 CHANDIGARH 1,73,917 \n29 ARUNACHAL PRADESH 1,40,041 \n30 THE DADRA AND NAGAR HAVELI AND DAMAN \nAND DIU 72,799 \n31 MIZORAM 58,089 \n32 GOA 51,663 \n33 ANDAMAN AND NICOBAR ISLANDS 28,410 \n34 LADAKH 28,388 \n35 SIKKIM 23,110 \n36 LAKSHADWEEP 2,411 \n  TOTAL 28,50,75,036 \n****** \n \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S11_U_148": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 148 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 02.02.2023\n \n \nINDUSTRIAL CORRIDOR/CLUSTER\n \n \n148.\n \n \nSHRI L.S. TEJASVI SURYA: \n \n \n \n \nWill the Minister of MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES be pleased to state:\n \n \n(a) \n \nwhether the Government proposes to introduce scheme for developing industrial \ncorridor or cluster only for the Micro, Small and Medium Enterprises (MSMEs) and if so, the \nde\ntails thereof;\n \n(b) \n \nthe policies and schemes available for entrepreneurs to develop clusters under PPP \nmodel;\n \n(c) \n \nthe Vendor Development Programmes (VDPs) conducted by the Government at \nNational and State level along with the outcome thereof during each o\nf the last three years;\n \n(d) \n \nthe details of the initiatives taken under VDPs by the leading PSUs in the Karnataka; \nand\n \n(e) \n \nthe steps taken by the Government to provide business opportunities to MSMEs to \nexpand their market base?\n \n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF STAT\nE FOR MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n(SHRI BHANU PRATAP SINGH VERMA)\n \n \n(a)\n:\n \nFor development of clusters, \nMinistry of MSME\n \nis implementing Micro and Small \nEnterprises \n–\n \nCluster Development Programme (MSE\n-\nCDP). The objective of the scheme is \nto enhance the productivity and competitiveness of Micro and Small Enterprises (MSEs) by \nextending financial assistance for establishment\n \nof Common Facility Centers (CFCs) in the \nexisting clusters\n. The scheme also extends support to project of State Government to establish \n/ upgrade the Industrial Areas / Estates / Flatted Factory Complexes.\n \nMSE\n-\nCDP is a demand \ndriven scheme wherein \nfinanci\nal assistance is provided as \nGovernment of India’s grant\n.\n \n \n(b)\n:\n \nThere is no scheme of Ministry of MSME to develop clusters under PPP model. \n \n \n(c)\n:\n \nDetails of State level & National level Vendor Development Programmes (VDPs) \norganized under Procurement and \nMarketing Support (PMS) Scheme of Ministry of MSME \nduring each of the last three years are tabulated below:\n-\n \n \n \nYear\n \nNumber of State level \nVendor Development \nProgrammes (SVDPs) \n \nNumber of National level \nVendor Development \nProgrammes (NVDPs)\n \n2019\n-\n20\n \n48\n \n10\n \n2020\n-\n21\n \n--\n \n--\n \n2021\n-\n22\n \n--\n \n01\n \n2022\n-\n23 (till \n3\nrd\n \nQtr)\n \n--\n \n10\n \n \n \n\n \n \n: 2 :\n \n \n \nOutcome: \n-\n \nThe Vendor Development Programmes (VDPs) have benefitted MSEs in \nfacilitating market linkages for effective implementation of the Public Procurement Policy \n(PPP). The details of overall \nProcurement by Central Ministries / Departments / CPSEs are\n \navailable on \nPublic Procurement Policy monitoring portal:\n \nhttps://sambandh.msme.gov.in/PPP_Index.aspx\n   \n \n \n(d)\n:\n \n \nTo enhance participation of Micro and Small Enterprises (MSEs) in procurements \nmade by CPSEs (Including CPSEs in Karnataka) 11,309 Vender Devel\nopment Programmes \nincluding 1,859 Vendor Development Programmes of SC/ST owned MSEs have been \norganized by CPSEs since 2019\n-\n20. The year\n-\nwise details are as under:\n \n \nS. No.\n \nFinancial Year\n \nNo. of VDPs for \nGen. MSEs\n \nNo. of VDPs for \nSC/ST owned MSEs\n \n1.\n \n2019\n-\n20\n \n2,280\n \n472\n \n2.\n \n2020\n-\n21\n \n2,517\n \n503\n \n3.\n \n2021\n-\n22\n \n2,527\n \n506\n \n4.\n \n2022\n-\n23 (as on \n30.01.2023)\n \n2,126\n \n378\n \nTotal\n \n9,450\n \n1,859\n \n \n(Source of Information: Sambandh portal of Ministry of MSME)\n \n \n(e)\n:\n \n \nThe Procurement and Marketing Support (PMS) Scheme of Ministry of MSME helps \nin enhancing the marketability of products and services of the MSMEs. The scheme extends \nbenefits to MSMEs for creating new market access initiatives like:\n \ni\n.\n \nOrganizing /participat\nion in national / international Trade Fairs / Exhibitions / \nMSME Expo, etc. held across the country.\n \nii\n.\n \nCreating awareness and educating MSMEs about importance / methods/ process of \npackaging in marketing, latest packaging technology, import\n-\nexport policy and\n \nprocedure, GeM portal, MSME Conclave, latest developments in international / \nnational trade and other subjects / topics relevant for market access developments \netc.\n \niii\n.\n \nIn order to provide an easy on\n-\nline access to all the procurement needs/demands \nfrom Gover\nnment Buyers, an on\n-\nline portal Government e\n-\nmarketplace (GeM) is in \nplace. This portal is meant for\n \nall \nthe vendors including MSMEs. \nAn MoU has \nbeen signed between Ministry of MSME and GeM for mobilizing maximum \nnumber of MSEs on GeM platform. Bulk mail w\nas sent to all UAM holders for \nregistering themselves at GeM portal for availing the benefits of the Public \nProcurement Policy.\n \n \n*****\n \n \n \n \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S11_U_149": "Page 1 of 2 \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 149 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 2nd February 2023 \n  \nImport of Oil \n \n149. SHRI SAPTAGIRI SANKAR ULAKA: \n \nपे\u0000ो\u0000लयम  और \u0000ाक ृ \u0000तक गैस मं\u0000ी  \n \nWill the Minister of PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the monthly oil import prices during the year 2021-22;  \n(b) whether the Government is deliberating on an import substitute product which is \npollution- free and cost-effective and if so, the details thereof; and  \n(c) if not, the reasons therefor?  \n \n        ANSWER  \n \nपे\u0000ो\u0000लयम  और \u0000ाक ृ \u0000तक गैस मं\u0000ालय  म\u0000 रा\u0000य  मं\u0000ी  (\u0000ी  रामे\u0000वर तेल\u0000 ) \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS \n(SHRI RAMESWAR TELI) \n         \n(a) The Indian basket of Crude Oil represents a derived basket comprising of Sour grade \n(Oman & Dubai average) and Sweet grade (Brent Dated) of Crude oil processed in Indian \nrefineries in the ratio of 75.62: 24.38 during 2021-22. The details of average monthly price per \nbarrel of Indian Basket of Crude oil during the 2021-22 is given below:  \nMonth \nAverage International Crude Oil \nPrices (Indian Basket) (in US$/bbl.)  \nApril,2021 63.40 \nMay,2021 66.95 \nJune,2021 71.98 \nJuly,2021 73.54 \nAugust,2021 69.80 \nSeptember,2021 73.13 \nOctober,2021 82.11 \nNovember,2021 80.64 \nDecember,2021 73.30 \nJanuary,2022 84.67 \nFebruary,2022 94.07 \nMarch,2022 112.87 \n2021-22 79.18 \nSource: PPAC \n\nPage 2 of 2 \n \n(b) & (c) Government has announced its aim of achieving 500 GW installed capacity from \nnon-fossil fuel based capacity (Hydro, Nuclear, Solar PV, Wind, Biomass etc.) by 2030. \nGovernment has also taken up development of National Gas Grid, City Gas Distribution \nNetworks to cover major demand centres across the country to provide clean and green fuel to \nPublic. Further, Government has launched National Biofuel Policy, 2018, to boost availability \nof biofuels in country and use of alternative clean fuels like ethanol, bio-diesel and bio-CNG \nthrough Ethanol Blending, Bio-diesel blending and Sustainable Alternative Towards \nAffordable Transportation (SATAT) initiative respectively with the objectives of reducing \nimport dependence, promote clean fuel and provide boost to agriculture. \n \nAs per Ministry of Power, significant addition to Thermal, Large Hydro and Nuclear Energy \ncapacity is also underway.  \n \n \n***** ",
    "ls|LS17_S11_U_15": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 15 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON FEBRUARY 02, 2023 \n \nFACILITIES TO STREET VENDORS \n \nNO. 15.  SHRI JAYADEV GALLA: \nSHRI RAM MOHAN NAIDU KINJARAPU: \n  \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS be p leased to \nstate: \n \na)  the list of urban vendors provided with identity ca rds during the year \n2021-22 under National Urban Livelihoods Mission (N ULM), State and \ndistrict-wise; \nb)  the list and the details regarding the proposed pro jects for \ninfrastructure development of vending zones in the country, State and \ndistrict-wise; \nc)  the status of projects sanctioned, type of projects , cost incurred, \ndeadlines and the reasons for delay, if any; \nd)  the details of areas in which training programmes a nd sessions on \nfinancial literacy for street vendors have been con ducted during the \nyear 2021-22; and \ne)  the number of street vendors who gained access to c redit, amount of \nfinancial assistance disbursed under Self Employmen t Programme-\nIndividual (SEP-I) component of NULM, State/ distri ct-wise, including \nAndhra Pradesh? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n(SHRI HARDEEP SINGH PURI) \n \n(a) to (e): A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n***** \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY PARTS (a) to (e) OF THE LOK \nSABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. 15 FOR ANSWER ON 02.02.2 023 \nREGARDING FACILITIES TO STREET VENDORS \n \n(a):  The Support to Urban Street Vendor (SUSV), on e of the \ncomponent of the Deendayal Antyodaya Yojana – Natio nal Urban \nLivelihoods Mission (DAY-NULM), complements the \nimplementation of the Street Vendors (Protection of  Livelihood \nand Regulation of Street Vending) Act, 2014by the S tates / Union \nTerritories (UTs). Under SUSV, financial support is  being \nprovided, \ninter-alia , for identification of street vendors, issuance \nof Identity Cards and development of vending infrastructure etc. \nDistrict-wise information of street vendors provide d with identity \ncards is not maintained at the level of Ministry of  Housing and \nUrban Affairs (MoHUA). However, State/UT-wise infor mation, as \nreceived from States/UTsfor year 2021-22, is at Annexure-I.  \n \n(b)&(c): The Street Vendors Act is being implemente d by respective \nStates/UTs by framing Rules, Scheme, Bye-laws and P lans for \nstreet vending as per provisions of the Act. As per  Section 21(1) \n of the Act, every local authority shall, in consul tation with the \nplanning authority and on the recommendations of th e Town \nVending Committee, once in every five years, prepar e a plan to \npromote the vocation of street vendors covering the  \nmatters which includes determination of vending zon es as \nrestriction-free-vending zones, restricted vending zones and no-\nvending zones based on local conditions in their re spective \njurisdictions.  \n \nUnder DAY-NULM, funds are provided to States/UTs in  a \nconsolidated manner. Out of total funds including state share, the \nStates/UTs can spend upto 5% of funds for implement ation of \nSUSV component including, inter-alia, development o f vending \ninfrastructure. Details of the projects for infrast ructure \ndevelopment of vending zones, state-wise & district -wise, is not \nmaintained centrally. As reported by States/UTs, a total of 13,403 \nvending zones have been identified as on 25.01.2023.\n \n \n\n(d): The guidelines of SUSV prescribe that Urban Lo cal Body (ULB) \nwill organize sessions on financial literacy for id entified street \nvendors with support of resource organization and f ield staff. The \ndetails of such sessions is not maintained at the level of MoHUA. \n \nMoHUA is also implementing Central Sector Scheme PM  Street \nVendor’s AtmaNirbhar Nidhi (PM SVANidhi) since June  01, 2020 \nto facilitate collateral free working capital loan, to street vendors \nto restart their businesses adversely impacted due to Covid-19 \npandemic. Digital integration of beneficiaries is a n integral \ncomponent of the Scheme. Beneficiaries are trained in debit and \ncredit transactions within a week of loan disbursem ent by the \nconcerned branch of the Bank. To provide further im petus to it, \n‘Main Bhi Digital’ campaign has been organised thri ce i.e. from \nJanuary 4 to February 28, 2021, July 1 to August 15 , 2021 and \nfrom September 9 to November 30, 2021. \n \n(e):  Self Employment Programme (SEP), a component under DAY-\nNULM, focuses on financial assistance to individual s / groups of \nurban poor for setting up gainful self-employment v entures/ \nmicro-enterprise, suited to their skills, training, aptitude and local \nconditions. Individual Micro-Enterprises and Group Enterprises \nare eligible for Bank loans upto \n\u00012 Lakh and \u000110 Lakh, \nrespectively. Linking of Self Help Groups (SHGs) wi th banks has \nbeen emphasized under the Mission. Interest subsidy , over and \nabove 7% rate of interest is paid to beneficiaries.  Further, an \nadditional 3 percent interest subvention is being p rovided to all \nwomen SHGs who repay their loan in time. \n \nDistrict-wise information of street vendors who rec eived credit \nlinkage under SEP is not maintained at the level of  MoHUA. \nHowever, State/UT-wise, including Andhra Pradesh, i nformation \nas received from States/UTs is at Annexure-II.\n \n \n***** \n  \n\n \nANNEXURE-I REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) of LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 15 TO BE ANSWERED ON FEBRUARY02, 2023. \n \nSTATE / UT-WISE NUMBER OF STREET VENDORS PROVIDED W ITH \nIDENTITY CARDS DURING YEAR 2021-22 \nS. No.  State/Union Territory No. of Street \nvendors  \n1 Andaman & Nicobar Islands 670  \n2 Andhra Pradesh 7,590  \n3 Assam 513  \n4 Bihar 35,185  \n5 Chhattisgarh 1,367  \n6 Haryana 5,621  \n7 Himachal Pradesh 91  \n8 Jammu & Kashmir 499  \n9 Jharkhand 1,495  \n10 Karnataka 8,023  \n11 Kerala 3,642  \n12 Madhya Pradesh 1,65,658  \n13 Maharashtra 3,033  \n14 Manipur 6,273  \n15 Meghalaya 30  \n16 Nagaland 47  \n17 NCT of Delhi 56,422  \n18 Odisha 2,417  \n19 Punjab 2,118  \n20 Rajasthan 3,236  \n21 Tamil Nadu 693  \n22 Telangana 43,162  \n23 Uttar Pradesh 29,785  \n24 Uttarakhand 3 \n  Total 3,77,573  \n \n  \n\nANNEXURE-II REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (e) of LOK  SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 15 TO BE ANSWERED ON FEBRUARY 02, 2023. \n \nSTATE / UT-WISE NUMBER OF STREET VENDORS RECEIVED C REDIT \nLINKAGE AND AMOUNT OF FINANCIAL ASSISTANCEDISBURSED  UNDER \nSELF EMPLOYMENT PROGRAMME (SEP) FROM01.04.2014 TO 25.01.2023 \nS. No. \n States/UTs Nos. of Street \nVendors  \nAmount disbursed \n(in lakhs) \n1 Andhra Pradesh 4,105                    2,529.79  \n2 Arunachal Pradesh 17                         30.40  \n3 Assam 26                         14.94  \n4 Bihar 360                       223.99  \n5 Chhattisgarh 403                       291.64  \n6 Gujarat 372                       351.82  \n7 Haryana 4                          3.45  \n8 Himachal Pradesh 81                         62.75  \n9 Jammu & Kashmir 4                           5.10  \n10 Jharkhand 298                       202.48  \n11 Karnataka 185                       202.01  \n12 Kerala 171                       153.27  \n13 Madhya Pradesh 3,298                    2,474.15  \n14 Maharashtra 1,889                    1,675.79  \n15 Meghalaya 1                           0.20  \n16 Mizoram 17                         25.23  \n17 Nagaland 1                           2.00  \n18 NCT of Delhi 6                           4.20  \n19 Odisha 126                         91.95  \n20 Puducherry 4                           1.15  \n21 Punjab 7                          4.08  \n22 Rajasthan 214                       199.41  \n23 Tamil Nadu 5,158                    1,306.99  \n24 Telangana 864                       508.77  \n25 Tripura 61                         34.47  \n26 Uttar Pradesh 41                         49.86  \n27 Uttarakhand 27                         26.11  \n28 West Bengal 20                         15.31  \n Total 17,760                  10,491.30  \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S11_U_150": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 150\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 02.02.2023\n \n \nSMALL AND COTTAGE INDUSTRIES\n \n \n150.\n \nSHRI SHANMUGA SUNDARAM K.: \n \n \nWill the Minister of MICRO, SMALL AND\n \nMEDIUM ENTERPRISES be pleased to state:\n \n \n(a) \n \nwhether the Government is aware of the fact that by encouraging small and cottage \nindustries in rural, tribal and semi\n-\nurban areas would create employment opportunities for \nlocals, thus preventing them from migrating to already crowded cities; \n \n(b) \n \nif so,\n \nthe details of the initiatives that have been taken and proposed to be taken by the \nGovernment in this regard; \n \n(c) \n \nthe funds allocated, disbursed and utilised for the same, State\n-\nwise during the last five \nyears and the current year; and \n \n(d) \n \nif not, th\ne reasons therefor?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n(SHRI BHANU PRATAP SINGH VERMA)\n \n \n(a)&(b):\n \nThe ‘Cottage Industry’ has not been defined under the Khadi\n \nand Village \nIndustries Commission Act, 1956. However, the broad contours of ‘Cottage Industry’ are \ncovered under the ‘Village Industry’ vertical under the purview of the Khadi and Village \nIndustries Commission (KVIC) and are broadly classified under six g\nroups for the purpose of \nimplementation of its programmes which are as follows: \n \n1\n.\n \nAgro Based & Food Processing Industry (ABFPI)\n \n2\n.\n \nMineral Based Industry (MBI)\n \n3\n.\n \nWellness & Cosmetics Industry\n \n4\n.\n \nHandmade Paper, Leather & Plastic Industry (HPLPI) \n \n5\n.\n \nRural Engineering \nand New Technology Industry (RENTI)\n \n6\n.\n \nService Industry \n \n \nMinistry of MSME is implementing\n \nPrime Minister’s Employment Generation \nProgramme (PMEGP) scheme, a credit\n-\nlinked subsidy programme since 2008\n-\n09 with KVIC \nas nodal agency at the national level for gen\nerating self\n-\nemployment opportunities through \nestablishment of micro enterprises, including village industries, throughout the country.\n \n \nKVIC also imparts training to rural and traditional artisans in various rural industries. \nIt provides tools and equipme\nnt and hand holding support through implementation of various \nschemes/programmes like Honey Mission for beekeeping industries, Kumbh\nar \nSashaktikaran \nProgramme for Pottery artisans, Leather Craft Programme for leather artisans etc.\n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n: 2 :\n \n \nEntrepreneurs Development Programme (EDP) training is imparted to beneficiaries \nwho have established new micro enterprises under PMEGP, through KVIC, Khadi and \nVillage Industries Boards (KVIBs) Training Centers as well as Accredited Training Centers \nrun by\n \nCentral Government, National Small Industries Corporation Limited (NSIC),\n \nNational \nInstitute for Entrepreneurship and Small Business Development (NIESBUD), National \nInstitute for Micro, Small and Medium Enterprises (NIMSME),\n \nIndian Inst\nitute of \nEntreprene\nurship\n \n(IIE)\n \nand their partner institutions under the administrative control of \nMinistry of MSME. State Governments, Banks, Rural Development and Self Employment \nTraining In\nstitutes (RUDSETI) reputed NGOs\n \nand other organizations/institutions, identified \nby\n \nthe Government from time to time also impart such training. \n \n \nMinistry of MSME has also launched ‘National Scheduled Caste Scheduled Tribe \nHub’ (NSSH) scheme in 2016, with an aim at capacity enhancement of SC\n-\nST entrepreneurs \nand promoting “entrepreneursh\nip culture” amongst the SC\n-\nST population. The scheme is \nempowering the SC/ST population to participate in the public procurement process and fulfill \nthe mandated target of 4% procurement from SC/ST enterprises under Public Procurement \nPolicy by the Ministr\nies, Departments and CPSEs. \n \n \nIn addition to the above, KVIC\n \nfacilitates marketing support by organizing District, \nState,\n \nNational level exhibitions where the institutions, entrepreneurs promoted by KVIC can \ndisplay and sell their products. Handholding support is being provided to unemployed youth \nto avail financial assistance from the Banks to set up their micro enterprises\n.\n \nOnline portal \nwas launched by KVIC for registration of ‘Khadi Certificate’ for new entrepreneurs.\n \n \n(c):\n \nState\n-\nwise performance, along with targets and achievements, under PMEGP during \nthe last five years and the current year (as on 31.12.2022) is placed \nat \nAnnexure\n. There is no \nState\n-\nwise allocation of fund under NSSH.\n \n \n(d):\n \nDoes not arise.\n \n \n* * * * *\n \n \n \n\nAnnexure\n \n \nAnnexure referred to in reply to part (c) of the Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No.150 \nfor answer on 02.02.2023\n \n \n \nState\n-\nwise performance, along wi\nth targets and achievements, under PMEGP during the last \nfive years and the current year (as on 31.12.2022).\n \n2017\n-\n18\n \n \n(Margin Money: Rs. in lakh, Project and Employment: in numbers)\n \nSr. \nNo.\n \nState/UT\n \nTarget\n \nAchievement\n \nProjects\n \nMargin \nMoney\n \nEmploym\nent\n \nProjects\n \nMargin \nMoney\n \nEmploym\nent\n \n1\n \nJammu Kashmir\n \n2386\n \n4772.84\n \n19088\n \n3753\n \n6913.15\n \n30024\n \n2\n \nHimachal Pradesh\n \n893\n \n1785.19\n \n7144\n \n886\n \n2042.54\n \n7088\n \n3\n \nPunjab\n \n1886\n \n3772.84\n \n15088\n \n1520\n \n3930.46\n \n12160\n \n4\n \nChandigarh (UT)\n \n100\n \n100.00\n \n800\n \n45\n \n90.07\n \n360\n \n5\n \nHaryana\n \n1886\n \n3772.84\n \n15088\n \n1718\n \n4167.00\n \n13744\n \n6\n \nDelhi\n \n100\n \n200.00\n \n800\n \n115\n \n150.65\n \n920\n \n7\n \nRajasthan\n \n2455\n \n4909.26\n \n19640\n \n1577\n \n4929.04\n \n12616\n \n8\n \nUttarakhand\n \n1067\n \n2133.95\n \n8536\n \n1613\n \n2880.98\n \n12904\n \n9\n \nUttar Pradesh\n \n6579\n \n13157.41\n \n52632\n \n5432\n \n16866.47\n \n43456