\nWarangal \n32.  TRIPURA 08 Dhalai, Gomati, North Tripura, Sepahijala, South Tripura, \nUnakoti, Khowai, West Tripura \n33.  UTTAR PRADESH 76 Agra, Aligarh, Ambedkar Nagar, Amethi, Amroha, Auriya, \nAzamgarh, Budaun, Baghpat, Bahraich, Ballia, Balrampur, \nBanda, Barabanki, Bareilly, Basti, Bhadohi, Bijnor, \nBulandshahar, Chandauli, Chitrakoot, Deoria, Etah, Etawah, \nFaizabad, Farrukh abad, Fatehpur, Firozabad, Gautam Buddha \nNagar, Ghaziabad, Ghazipur, Gonda, Gorakhpur, Hamirpur, \nHapur, Hardoi, Hathras, Jalaun, Jaunpur, Jhansi, Kannauj, \nKanpur Dehat, Kanpur Nagar, Kanshiramnagar, Kaushambi, \nKushinagar, Lakhimpur Kheri, Lalitpur, Lucknow , \nMaharajganj, Mahoba, Mainpuri, Mathura, Mau, Meerut, \nMirzapur, Moradabad, Muzaffarnagar, Pilibhit, Pratapgarh, \nPrayagraj, Raebareli, Rampur, Saharanpur, Sambhal, Sant Kabir \nNagar, Shahjahanpur, Shamali, Shravasti, Siddharthnagar, \nSitapur-I, Sitapur-II, Sonbhadra, Sultanpur, Unnao, Varanasi \n34.  UTTARAKHAND 13 Almora, Bageshwar, Chamoli, Champawat, Dehradun, \nHaridwar, Nainital, Pauri Garhwal, Pithoragarh, Rudraprayag, \nTehri Garhwal, Udham Singh Nagar, Uttarkashi \n35.  WEST BENGAL 20 Alipurduar, Bankura, Birbhum, Coochbehar, Dakshin Dinajpur, \nDarjeeling, East Medinipur, Hooghly, Howrah, Jalpaiguri,  \nMalda, Murshidabad, North 24 Parganas, Nadia, Purulia, South \n24 Parganas – I, South 24 Parganas - II, North Dinajpur, West \nBardhman, West Medinipur \nTOTAL 661  \n \nThe State of Tamil Nadu is yet to accept the Navodaya Vidyalaya Scheme. \n \n***** \n  \n\n \n \nAnnexure – II \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) to (f) OF LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 42 FOR 06.02.2023 RAISED BY SHRI N. REDDEPPA \nAND SHRI NAMA NAGESWARA RAO , HON’BLE MEMBERS OF PARLIAMENT \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 REGARDING SETTING UP OF JAWAHAR \nNAVODAYA VIDYALAYAS \n \nList of 80 newly created/carved out/uncovered districts, as on 31.01.2023, where there are \nno JNVs \n \nS. \nNo. State \nNo. of newly \ncreated/carved \nout/uncovered \ndistricts \nNewly created/carved out/uncovered districts  \n \n1.  Andhra \nPradesh \n14 Alluriseetharama Raju, Anakapalli, Ananthapur, Bapatla, \nChittoor, Dr. Ambedkar Konaseema, East Godavari, Guntur, \nNandyal, NTR, Parvathipuram Manyam, Thirupathi, West \nGodavari, YSR \n2.  Arunachal \nPradesh \n08 Upper Subansiri, Kradadi, Lepa Rada, Lower Siang, Lohit, \nPakke-Kessang, Shi-Yomi, Siang \n3.  Assam 06 Sonitpur, Charaideo, Hojai, Majuli, South Salmara \nMankachar, West Karbianglong  \n4.  Chhattisgarh \n06 Gaurela-Pendra-Marwahi, Khairagarh-Chhuikhadan-Gandai, \nMahendragarh-Chirmiri-Bharatpur (MCB) , Mohla-Manpur-\nAmbagarh-Chouki, Sakti, Sarangarh-Bilaigarh \n5.  Haryana 01 Charkhi Dadri \n6.  Karnataka 01 Bellary \n7.  Madhya \nPradesh \n01 Niwari \n8.  Maharashtra 01 Thane \n9.  Manipur 07 Jiribam, Thoubal, Kamjong, Kangpokpi, Noney, Pherzawl, \nTengnoupal \n10.  Meghalaya 01 Eastern West Khasi Hills \n11.  Mizoram 03 Hnahthial, Champhai, Saitual \n12.  Nagaland 02 Noklak, Dimapur \n13.  Punjab 01 Malerkotla \n14.  Sikkim 02 East Sikkim, West Sikkim \n15.  Telangana \n23 Jagityal, Jai Shankar Bhoopalapalli, Janagaon, Jogulamba \nGadwal, Nizamabad, Kothagudem Bhadradri, Adilabad, \nMahaboobabad, Mancherial, Medchal, Mulugu, \nMahaboobnagar, Narayanpet, Nirmal, Peddapalli, Sanga \nReddy, Medak, Ranjana Sirisilla, Suryap et, Vikarabad, \nWanaparthy, Hanumankonda, Yadadri (Bhongiri) \n16.  West Bengal 03 East Bardhman, Jhargram, Kalimpong \nTOTAL 80  \n \nJNVs have not been sanctioned in Central Delhi, New Delhi, Mumbai, Mumbai Suburban, \nHyderabad and Kolkata districts as these districts have 100% urban  population. Further, the \nState of Tamil Nadu is yet to accept the Navodaya Vidyalaya Scheme. \n \n***** ",
    "ls|LS17_S11_U_43": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. : 43\n(To be answered on the 2nd February 2023)\n \nDISINVESTMENT OF AIRPORTS\n \n \nWill the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION\n \n(a) whether the Government has proposed disinvestment of airports in the country\nand if so, the details thereof;\n(b) the complete list of all the airports given to the private players and the income\ngenerated from each airport during the last five years; and\n(c) the funds spent by the Government for the construction of these airports?\n \nANSWER\n \nMinister of State in the Ministry of CIVIL AVIATION\n \n \n(a): As per National Monetization Pipeline (NMP), 25 airports of Airports\nAuthority of India (AAI) have been earmarked for leasing over the years 2022 to\n2025.\n \n(b): AAI has leased out eight of its airports namely, Delhi, Mumbai, Ahmedabad,\nGuwahati, Jaipur, Lucknow, Mangaluru and Thiruvananthapuram through Public\nPrivate Partnership (PPP) for operation, management and development on long\nterm lease basis.\n \nOut of these, Delhi and Mumbai airports were handed over in 2006. During the last\nfive years i.e. from 2017-18 to 2021-22, AAI has received revenues of approximately\nRs. 5500 crore from Delhi Airport and Rs. 5174 crore from Mumbai Airport.\n \nThe 06 Airports recently awarded under PPP viz. Mangaluru, Lucknow,\nAhmedabad, Guwahati, Jaipur and Thiruvananthapuram have been handed over\nto the Concessionaires on 31.10.2020, 02.11.2020, 07.11.2020, 08.10.2021, 11.10.2021\nand 14.10.2021 respectively. Till December, 2022, AAI has received concession fees\nof approximately Rs. 824 crore from the concessionaires for these six airports.\nFurther, AAI has also received an amount of approximately Rs.2349 crore in the\nform of upfront fee towards the capital expenditure incurred by AAI at these\nairports.\n \n(c): The ongoing works undertaken by AAI at the six airports i.e. Ahmedabad,\n43. SHRI ANURAG SHARMA\n be pleased to state:-\n (GEN. (DR) V. K. SINGH (RETD))\n\nLucknow, Mangaluru, Jaipur, Guwahati & Thiruvananthapuram were not\naffected during the PPP process i.e. from March, 2018 till handing over the airports\nto the PPP Partner. During this period, AAI had spent an amount of approximately\nRs. 1970 crore towards capital works at these six airports. This Capital expenditure\nincurred by AAI is payable by the PPP Partner. \n \n*****",
    "ls|LS17_S11_U_44": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FINANCE \nDEPARTMENT OF REVENUE \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO-44 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON MONDAY, THE 6TH FEBRUARY, 2023 \n \nGST COMPENSATION TO ANDHRA PRADESH \n \n44. SHRI BALASHOWRY VALLABHANENI: \n             \nWill the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether nearly Rs. 690 crore GST compensation due is yet to be released by the \nGovernment to the State of Andhra Pradesh and if so, the details thereof and the \ntime by which the said compensation amount is likely to be released by the \nGovernment; \n \n(b) whether almost all States have r eportedly demanded for extending the period of \nGST compensation for five more years and if so, the details thereof and the reaction \nof the Government thereto; and \n \n(c) whether the issue of extension of GST compensation period has been placed \nbefore the GST  Council and if so, the details thereof and the response of the GST \nCouncil thereto? \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF FINANCE  \n(MS. NIRMALA SITHARAMAN) \n \n \n(a) to (c):  A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n*****\n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION \nNO. 44 FOR ANSWER ON 6TH FEBRUARY, 2023 \n \n \n(a):- As per provision of GST (Compensation to States) Act, 2017 and taking into account ₹ \n1.10 lakh crore for FY 2020-21 and ₹ 1.59 lakh crore for FY 2021-22 released to States/UTs \nas back to back loan in lieu of GST compensation, entire GST compensation dues of \nStates/UTs including State of Andhra Pradesh has been cleared by Central Government for \nthe period up to 31.05.2022. During 2022 -23, Central Government decided to rel ease full \ncompensation up to 31.05.2022 despite insufficient amount in the Compensation Fund, by \nmeeting the required balance from its own resources to the extent of ₹ 62,000 crore in May \n2022 and ₹ 17,000 crore again in November 2022, to assist the States  in managing their \nresources. Taking into account back to back loan of ₹ 2311 crore in FY 2020 -21 and ₹ 3272 \ncrore in FY 2021 -22 released to Andhra Pradesh in lieu of GST compensation and release \nof bimonthly GST compensation from July, 2017 to June, 2022,  only ₹ 689 crore is pending \nto Andhra Pradesh toward GST compensation for the period April -June, 2022. This pending \namount would be released from the compensation Fund wherein monthly cess collection is \nbeing transferred and credited.  \n(b) & (c): - A few S tates have requested for extension of payment of GST compensation \nbeyond transition period of five years. As per section 18 of the Constitution (One Hundred \nand First Amendment) Act, 2016, compensation to the States for loss of revenue arising on \naccount of implementation of the goods and services tax for a period of five years  can be \nprovided by a law made by Parliament. Central Government is committed for payment of \nGST compensation to States/UTs for five years as per law enacted by the Parliament \naccording to the Constitutional provision. \n \n****** \n \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S11_U_45": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. : 45\n(To be answered on the 2nd February 2023)\n \nDIGI YATRA POLICY\n \n \nWill the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION\n \n(a) whether the Government has launched Digi Yatra Policy and if so, the\ndetails including aims and objectives thereof;\n(b) the financial allocation for implementing the said policy;\n(c) the details of the benefit(s) accrued to the people from the said policy;\n(d) whether the Government has launched any awareness programme about\nDigi Yatra Policy and if so, its outcome thereof;\n(e) the details of the airports which were awarded work for implementation of\nBiometric Boarding System by Airports Authority of India;\n(f) whether any time bound action plan is proposed to cover all airports of the\ncountry under the above scheme and if so, the details thereof; and\n(g) the steps taken/proposed to be taken by the Government for digital\ntransformation of the aviation sector?\n \nANSWER\n \nMinister of State in the Ministry of CIVIL AVIATION\n \n \n(a) to (e): Digi Yatra policy is an initiative launched by Ministry of Civil\nAviation for biometric boarding system using Facial Recognition Technology.\n45. SHRIMATI MANJULATA MANDAL\nDR. SUBHASH RAMRAO BHAMRE\nDR. DNV SENTHILKUMAR S.\nSHRI KULDEEP RAI SHARMA\nSHRI C.N. ANNADURAI\nSHRI SUNIL DATTATRAY TATKARE\nSHRI DHANUSH M. KUMAR\nDR. AMOL RAMSING KOLHE\nSHRIMATI SUPRIYA SULE\nSHRI M.K.RAGHAVAN\n be pleased to state:-\n (GEN. (DR) V. K. SINGH (RETD))\n\nIt aims at providing seamless and hassle-free experience for passengers at\nairports. Its main objective is to enhance passenger experience by eliminating\nthe need for verification of ticket and ID at multiple touch points and to\nachieve better throughput through existing infrastructure using a Digital\nFramework. The project is under implementation stage.The expenditure on\nimplementation of Digi Yatra is done by Airport Operators. Ministry of Civil\nAviation does not provide any budgetary support for its implementation. To\ngive wide publicity to Digi Yatra, Airport Operators and Airline Operators\nare doing in-flight announcements, publicity through boarding passes,\nproviding help desk support and displaying banner and films etc. at the\nairports. Publicity is also being done through Social Media.\nAirports Authority of India (AAI) has awarded the work for implementation\nof Biometric Boarding System at Kolkata, Pune, Vijayawada and Varanasi\nairports.\n \n(f) and (g): Digi Yatra is to be implemented across airports in a phased\nmanner. In first phase, Digi Yatra has been launched on 01.12.2022 at Delhi,\nBangalore and Varanasi Airports for providing passengers contactless,\npaperless check-in and boarding process based on facial biometrics. The\nimplementation of Digi Yatra under phase I is also planned at Kolkata, Pune,\nVijayawada and Hyderabad Airports by March 2023 and across various\nairports in the country in phased manner.\n \nDigital transformation of aviation sector is an ongoing endeavour. As per\nrequirement, the digitisation of various processes in aviation sector is\nundertaken.\n \n*****",
    "ls|LS17_S11_U_46": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 46 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON FEBURARY 02, 2023 \n \nACCIDENTS DUE TO NEGLECTED DRAINS \n \nNO. 46.  SHRI THIRUNAVUKKARASAR SU: \nSHRI RAVNEET SINGH BITTU: \n \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether it is a fact that there have been unnat ural deaths/grave injuries of \nindividuals due to fall in open, neglected drains i n States/UTs, including \nthose under the land owning agency Delhi Developmen t Authority (DDA) in \nDelhi; \n(b) if so, the details of such incidents, State/UT wise, especially in Delhi, Tamil \nNadu and Punjab; \n(c) the details of compensation paid to the family members or the individuals \nwho are victims of such incidents; \n(d) the details of criminal cases filed and action taken against officers \naccountable in such bodies in States/UTs and DDA, State/UT-wise; and \n(e) whether the Government is taking any measures t o minimize such accidents \ndue to open, neglected drains and if so, the detail s thereof and if not, the \nreasons therefor? \n \n…2/- \n \n \n\n-2- \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE \n MINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n(SHRI KAUSHAL KISHORE) \n(a)&(b): The instant matter is a State subject. How ever, as per the \ninformation furnished by National Crime Records Bur eau (Statistical \nBranch), during 2019 - 2021, there have been 378 ac cidental deaths due to \nfall into manhole/sewer including Delhi, Tamil Nadu, and Punjab. Details are \nat ANNEXURE–I. Further, Delhi Development Authority  (DDA) has also \ninformed that no such incident has occurred. \n(c)& (d): The instant matter is a State subject. As  such no information/ \ndetails are maintained. \n(e): Ministry of Housing and Urban Affairs has publ ished a Manual on \nStorm Water Drainage Systems, 2019 whereby safety &  social safeguard \nrequirements have been mentioned. Many State(s) and Central Government \nagencies have also taken various measures to minimi ze such accidents \ndue to open, neglected drains.  \n***** \n  \n\nANNEXURE-I REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a)& (b) OF  LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 46 REGARDING “ACCIDENTS DUE TO NEGLECTED \nDRAINS”, DUE FOR ANSWER IN THE LOK SABHA ON 02 FEBURARY, 2023. \n \nState/UT-wise Number of Accidental Deaths due to Fall into Manhole/Sewer# during \n2019-2021 \nS.No. \n State/UT 2019 2020 2021 \n1 Andhra Pradesh 0 0 1 \n2 Arunachal Pradesh 0 0 0 \n3 Assam 0 1 0 \n4 Bihar 6 4 10 \n5 Chhattisgarh 0 0 1 \n6 Goa 0 0 1 \n7 Gujarat 30 23 17 \n8 Haryana 0 2 3 \n9 Himachal Pradesh 0 2 0 \n10 Jharkhand 0 0 5 \n11 Karnataka 0 0 1 \n12 Kerala 2 3 2 \n13 Madhya Pradesh 1 3 9 \n14 Maharashtra 19 16 19 \n15 Manipur 0 0 0 \n16 Meghalaya 0 0 5 \n17 Mizoram 0 0 0 \n18 Nagaland 0 0 0 \n19 Odisha 4 4 8 \n20 Punjab 5 8 7 \n21 Rajasthan 13 49 7 \n22 Sikkim 0 0 2 \n23 Tamil Nadu 1 4 3 \n24 Telangana 1 3 4 \n25 Tripura 0 0 6 \n26 Uttar Pradesh 16 14 12 \n27 Uttarakhand 2 0 0 \n28 West Bengal 0 0 0 \nTOTAL STATE(S) 100 136 123 \n29 A & N Islands 0 0 0 \n30 Chandigarh 0 0 0 \n31 D&N Haveli and Daman&Diu @ + 0 0 0 \n32 Delhi UT  2 6 3 \n33 Jammu & Kashmir @ * 0 0 8 \n34 Ladakh @  0 0 \n35 Lakshadweep 0 0 0 \n\n36 Puducherry 0 0 0 \nTOTAL UT(S) 2 6 11 \nTOTAL (ALL INDIA) 102 142 134 \nSource: Accidental Deaths & suicide in India, National Crime Records Bureau  \n(Statistical Branch) \n‘+’ Combined data of erstwhile Dadra & Nagar Haveli And Daman & Diu (UT) \nduring 2019 \n‘*’ Data of erstwhile JAMMU & KASHMIR State Including LADAKH during \n2019 \n‘@’ Data of newly created Union territory \n# Word \"Sewer\" included in year 2020 \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S11_U_47": "LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 47 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 02 FEBRUARY, 2023 \n \nFunctioning of PSUs \n  \n47. SHRI LAVU SRIKRISHNA DEVARAYALU: \n \n                                  पे\u0000ोिलयम और \u0000ाक ृितक गैस मं\u0000ी  \nWill the Minister of PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  the details of Public Sector Undertakings (PSUs) functioning under the administrative control of the \nGovernment; \n \n(b)  the details of sanctioned posts, grade/category wise of each of the PSUs under administrative control \nof this Government, PSU-wise;  \n \n(c)  the details of contract and outsourced workers, grade/category-wise and the details of contract and \noutsourced workers working for more than two years; \n \n(d)  the details of vacancies as of 1st January, 2023 in each PSU, grade/category-wise; and \n \n(e)        the steps taken by the Government to fill in the above vacancies since 1st January, 2022,PSUwise? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nपे\u0000ोिलयम और \u0000ाक ृितक गैस मं\u0000ालय  म\u0000 रा\u0000यमं\u0000ी  \n(\u0000ी  रामे\u0000वर तेल\u0000) \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PETROLEUM & NATURAL GAS \n(SHRI RAMESWAR TELI) \n \n(a):  The details of Public Sector Undertakings (PSUs) of Ministry of Petroleum and Natural Gas \n(MoP&NG), functioning under the administrative control of the Government are as under : \n \nS.No. Public Sector Undertakings \n1.  Indian Oil Corporation Limited (IOCL) \n2.  Oil and Natural Gas Corporation Limited (ONGC) \n3.  GAIL (India) Limited  (GAIL) \n4.  Bharat Petroleum Corporation Limited (BPCL) \n5.  Hindustan Petroleum Corporation Limited  (HPCL) \n6.  Oil India Limited (OIL) \n7.  Engineers India Limited  (EIL) \n8.  Balmer Lawrie & Co. Limited  (BLL) \n9.  Chennai Petroleum Corporation Limited  (CPCL) \n10.  Brahmaputra Cracker and Polymer Limited  (BCPL) \n11.  Numaligarh Refinery Limited   (NRL) \n12.  Mangalore Refinery and Petrochemicals Limited (MRPL) \n \n\n \n(b)  As per the data received from these PSUs, the details of sanctioned posts, grade/category wise of \neach of the PSUs under administrative control of MoP&NG are at Annexure. \n \n(c)  As per the data received from these PSUs, the details of contract and outsourced workers, \ngrade/category-wise and the details of contract and outsourced workers working for more than two years are \nat Annexure. \n \n(d)  As per the data received from these PSUs, the details of vacancies as of 1st January, 2023 in each \nPSU, grade/category-wise are at Annexure. \n \n(e)   Vacancies are being filled up in phased manner in the PSUs under the Mission Recruitment Mode of the \nGovernment. As on 20 th January, 2023, 3641 appointment letters have been offered by these PSUs against \nthe vacancy of 5460. \n \n \n \n**** \n\n \nANNEXUREREFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (b) OF LOK SABHA UNSTARRED QUESTION \nNO. 47 ASKED BY SHRI LAVU SRIKRISHNA DEVARAYALU REGARDING ‘FUNCTIONING OF \nPSUs’ TO BE ANSWERED ON 2nd FEBRUARY, 2023 \n \n1. Indian Oil Corporation Limited(IOCL) \n \n2. Oil and Natural Gas Corporation Limited(ONGC) \nONGC does not have defined sanctioned strength. The recruitment of new manpower is done as per the \nmanpower planning vis a vis requirement as per the dynamic business scenario. The manpower position \n(grade-wise and category-wise) as on 01/01/2023 is given below: \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n3. Gas Authority of India Limited (GAIL) \nThe details of 5859 sanctioned posts as on 31.03.2022 in GAIL (India) Limited: \nGrade Total \nExecutive 4741 \nNon-Executive 1118 \n \n4. Bharat Petroleum Corporation Limited(BPCL) \nCategory  Sanctioned strength \nExecutive 5735 \nNon-executive 3179 \n \n5. Hindustan Petroleum Corporation Limited (HPCL) \n  Group A Group C \nSanctioned Strength  6327 2375 \n \n6. Oil  India Limited (OIL) \nGROUP CATEGORY Total \nA & B Officers 1814 \nC & D Non-Officers 5638 \n \n7. Engineers India Limited (EIL) \n303  posts are sanctioned for 3 levels as below: \nLevel No. of Posts \n20  20 \n19  62 \n18 221 \n \nExecutives Non-Executives Total \nSanctioned \nStrength \nPerson \nin \nPlace \nVacancies Sanctioned \nStrength \nPerson \nin Place Vacancies Sanctioned \nStrength \nPerson \nin Place Vacancies \nB C D E F G H = B+E I = C+F J = D+G \n18657 18630 27 12776 12692 84 31433 31322 111 \nGroup Grand Total \nGroup A 15483 \nGroup B 283 \nGroup C 9064 \nGroup D 1395 \n\n \n8. Balmer Lawrie & Co. Limited  (BLL) \n \nCategory Grade Sanctioned \nGroup A \nE0 59 \nE1 135 \nE2 101 \nE3 73 \nE4 68 \nE5 55 \nE6 28 \nE7 18 \nE8 4 \nED 1 \nGroup B O1 75 \nO2 113 \nGroup C Clerk 41 \nGroup D \nLabour 87 \nSub-Staff 87 \n \n9. Chennai Petroleum Corporation Limited (CPCL) \nThe details of sanctioned posts are as under:  \n \nGroup Sanctioned Strength  \nA&B 672 \nC 736 \n \n \n10. Brahmaputra Cracker and Polymer Limited (BCPL) \nThe details of sanctioned posts are as under:  \nSl \nno  Grade  Total Sanctioned Post  \n1 S-0 20 \n2 S-3 129 \n3 S-5 60 \n4 E-2 347 \n5 E-3 77 \n6 E-4 38 \n7 E-5 16 \n8 E-6 7 \n9 E-7 5 \n10 E-8 1 \n \n\n \n11. Numaligarh Refinery Limited (NRL) \nSr. No. DPE grade Sanctioned Manpower Strength \n(No.) \nCategory – A \n1 E-1 to E-5 407 \n2 E-6 94 \n3 E-7 89 \n4 E-8 69 \nCategory - A Total                                         659 \nCategory – B                                                       0 \nCategory- C/D Total                                      490 \n \n \n12. Mangalore Refinery and Petrochemicals Limited (MRPL) \nCategory Sanctioned Posts \nGroup - A 1228 \nGroup – B Nil \nGroup – C & D 1585 \n \n \n****\n\n \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (c) OF LOK SABHA UNSTARRED QUESTION \nNO. 47 ASKED BY SHRI LAVU SRIKRISHNA DEVARAYALU REGARDING ‘FUNCTIONING OF \nPSUs’ TO BE ANSWERED ON 2nd FEBRUARY, 2023 \n \n1. Indian Oil Corporation Limited (IOCL) \nOn an average, 88,000 contract workers are being engaged at IOCL, out of which approx. 31,413 are \nengaged in Project/Construction-related activities, 14,276 in Security-related jobs & rest approx..42,311 \nare being engaged in other activities including repair, maintenance, housekeeping, canteen, etc. \n \n2. Oil and Natural Gas Corporation Limited (ONGC) \nThe details of contract employees as on 01.08.2022 is as mentioned under: \nSl. No. Category Number of Employees \n1. Tenure Based Field Executives 56 \n2. Tenure Based Field Operators 477 \n3. Tenure Based Field Worker 01 \n4. Medical Officer on Contract 225 \n5. Paramedical Staff on Contract 141 \n6. Marine Security Supervisor 12 \n TOTAL 912 \n \nThe details of outsourced workers, working for more than two years is not available as contract labours \nare the employees of the contractors who deploys contract labours for execution of contract awarded by \nONGC. \n3. Gas Authority of India Limited (GAIL) \nThe total number of contract & outsourced workers deployed through various contractors under various \nservice contracts in GAIL is around 15876.No grade-wise or category-wise data of contract & \noutsourced workers is maintained in GAIL. Contract(s) for various services in GAIL are generally \nawarded for a period of 2 years. Accordingly, the respective contractor / service provider deploys their \ncontract workers for ensuring the services under such respective contracts. \n \n4. Bharat Petroleum Corporation Limited (BPCL) \n \nCategory  Number \nNon-project 16660 \nProject Contracts 1900 \nTotal 18560* \n*Total number of Contract Labour engaged in FY2021-22. \n \nBPCL, being a PAN India company, has office / operating establishments across the Country. Contract \nlabour are engaged by contractors across the Country, for non-core, sporadic and peripheral nature of \njobs as per “Contract for Services”. The number is dynamic and changes depending on projects/ works \nbeing undertaken. The contract labour in BPCL are engaged primarily in the areas of Projects \n(Engineering projects, Civil works etc.), Maintenance jobs (Tank Repairs, Painting, Valve repairs, Heat \nexchangers, AC plants, Pumps, other equipment(s) etc.), & in other peripheral activities such as \nSecurity, Housekeeping, Catering services etc. \n\n5. Hindustan Petroleum Corporation Limited (HPCL) \nAt HPCL, contract workers are engaged in non-core activities like housekeeping, security, canteen, \ngardening, maintenance, project related activities, etc. through contractor after following due tendering \nprocess and in adherence to applicable labour legislations. The contractor for performing the contractual \nobligations engages his own workmen. Since the engagement of workmen is within the purview of \ncontractor (as the employer), HPCL separately doesn't maintain such data with regard to continuity of \nworkmen. Details of Contract Labour engaged through Contractors at HPCL as on 3 1 .12.2022 is as \nunder: \nDivision Projects Non- Projects Total \nMarketing 632 15306 15938 \nMumbai Refinery 324 2590 2914 \nVisakh Refinery 10038 5285 15323 \nTotal 10994 23181 34175 \n \n6. Oil India Limited (OIL) \nAs far as Oil India Limited (OIL) is concerned, There are no contract or outsourced workers engaged for \nmore than two years. \n \n7. Engineers India Limited (EIL) \nNo. of Contract Workers- NIL.  \nNo. of Outsourced Workers - 287  \nNo. of Contract/Outsourced Workers, working for more than 2 years- NIL \n \n8. Balmer Lawrie & Co. Limited  (BLL) \nThe details are given as under: \nCategory Grade Sanctioned Working for more than 2 years \nContractual \n(Fixed Term \nContract) \nFTE0 37 37 \nFTE1 30 18 \nFTE2 17 12 \nFTE3 10 7 \nFTE4 2 1 \nFTE5 3 3 \nFTE6 1 1 \nFTE7 1 1 \nFTET 5 5 \nFTE8 0 0 \nFTO1 72 54 \nFTO2 57 40 \nDirect \nContract   31 0 \nOutsourced   392 357 \n \n \n9. Chennai Petroleum Corporation Limited (CPCL) \nNo Contracts and Out-Sourced workers are working in CPCL directly. \n \n \n \n \n \n\n10. Brahmaputra Cracker and Polymer Limited  (BCPL) \nThere are approximately 1800 contract workers across BCPL operational locations engaged under \nvarious contractors undertaking various contractual jobs at BCPL. The maximum period of contracts is \nfrom 1-2 years, hence no contractual workers engaged under any contract/s for more than two years. \n \n11. Numaligarh Refinery Limited (NRL) \n \n As on 1st January, 2023 Working for more than 2 years \nContract workers 18 11 \nOutsourced workers 1431 1131 \n \n12. Mangalore Refinery and Petrochemicals Limited (MRPL) \nMRPL does not hire directly any contract workers. In MRPL, contracts are awarded for specific jobs in \nthe Refinery as per the terms and conditions of the work order/contract.   \n \n**** \n\nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (d) OF LOK SABHA UNSTARRED QUESTION \nNO. 47 ASKED BY SHRI LAVU SRIKRISHNA DEVARAYALU REGARDING ‘FUNCTIONING OF \nPSUs’ TO BE ANSWERED ON 2nd FEBRUARY, 2023 \n \n  Description Vacancy status till June 2023 \nMRPL \nExecutive 140 \nNon-Executive 122 \nOIL Executive 51 \nNon-Executive 179 \nCPCL Executive 0 \nNon-Executive 15 \nEIL Executive 193 \nNon-Executive 60 \nNRL Executive 23 \nNon-Executive 55 \nONGC Executive 1669 \nNon-Executive 922 \nHPCL Executive 382 \nNon-Executive 186 \nBCPL Executive 66 \nNon-Executive 22 \nBalmer Lawrie & Co. Ltd Executive 68 \nNon-Executive 0 \nGAIL Executive 737 \nNon-Executive 282 \nBPCL Executive 177 \nNon-Executive 0 \nIOC Executive 27 \nNon-Executive 84 \nTOTAL Executive 3533 \n  Non-Executive 1927 \n  TOTAL 5460* \n \n*As on 20 January 2023, 3641 appointment letters have already been offered by these PSUs. \n \n**** \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S11_U_48": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 48 \nANSWERED ON 02.02.2023 \n \nBASIC CUSTOMS DUTY ON IMPORTED SOLAR MODULES \n \n48. SHRI DUSHYANT SINGH \n \n(a) whether it is a fact that the Basic Customs Duty (BCD) of 40 per cent on imported solar \nmodules has made private and PSU solar projects unviable due to increased costs; \n(b) if so, the details thereof, project-wise; \n(c) the amount of customs revenue lost due to solar developers using the \"Project Import\" \nloophole to avoid paying BCD; and \n(d) the details of the increase in indigenous production and sale of solar modules since the \nimplementation of BCD and the announcement of the PLI scheme, State/UT-wise? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF NEW & RENEWABLE ENERGY AND POWER  \n(SHRI R.K. SINGH) \n \n(a) & (b) No, Sir. The Government had announced imposition of Basic Customs Duty on import \nof solar PV cells and modules with effect from 01.04.2022, well in advance, on 09.03.2021 so \nthat the developers of solar power projects wherein the last date of bid submission was on or after \n09.03.2021, duly take into account, the impact of BCD with effect from 01.04.2022, while \nsubmitting their bids. \n \n(c)  Project Imports Regulations, 1986 apply f or assessment and clearance of the goods falling \nunder heading No. 98.01 of the First Schedule to the Customs Tariff Act, wherein, the rate of Basic \nCustoms Duty (BCD) is 7.5% with effect from 01.10.2022. The imports under the said regulations \nare governed by the terms and conditions specified therein and thus, it is not correct to label the \nsame as a loophole to avoid paying BCD.  \n \n(d) As per the Approved List of Models and Manufacturers (ALMM), in respect of solar PV \nmodules, issued by the Ministry of New & Renewable Energy (MNRE), the installed capacity of \nsolar PV module manufacturing (enlisted in ALMM) was about 8.2 GW in March 2021, which \nhas increased to about 21.7 GW in January 2023. The details of State -wise solar PV module \nmanufacturing capacity as per ALMM List of March 2021 and as per ALMM List of January 2023, \nis at Annexure-I. Solar PV module manufacturers are not required to report their production and \nsales data to Ministry of New & Renewable Energy (MNRE), and therefore, this data is not \navailable with MNRE. \n \n***** \n  \n\nAnnexure-I \n \nAnnexure-I referred to in reply to part (d) of Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No. 48 \nreplied on 02.02.2023 \n \nSl.  \nNo. \nState/ Union \nTerritory \nInstalled Solar PV \nModule Manufacturing \nCapacity as per ALMM \nList of  \nMarch 2021 \n(in MW per annum) \nInstalled Solar PV \nModule Manufacturing \nCapacity as per ALMM \nList of  \nJanuary 2023 \n(in MW per annum) \n \n1 Andhra Pradesh 0 14 \n2 Chhattisgarh 125 125 \n3 Goa 0 60 \n4 Gujarat 3895 11072 \n5 Haryana 240 591 \n6 Himachal Pradesh 0 31 \n7 Jharkhand 0 25 \n8 Karnataka 1000 2229 \n9 Maharashtra 200 915 \n10 Odisha 0 20 \n11 Rajasthan 0 161 \n12 Tamil Nadu 0 972 \n13 Telangana 1262 2411 \n14 Uttar Pradesh 0 1061 \n15 Uttarakhand 0 228 \n16 West Bengal 1460 1766 \n TOTAL 8182 21681 \n \n \n \n  ",
    "ls|LS17_S11_U_49": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TOURISM \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.*49 \nANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \nDEVELOPMENT OF ECO-TOURISM CIRCUIT \n \n*49. SHRI RAJENDRA AGRAWAL:  \n \nWill the Minister of TOURISM be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the details of eco -tourism circuit devel oped under Swadesh \nDarshan Scheme (SDS) in the country including Uttar Pradesh;  \n(b) the funds sanctioned for the said circuit in last three years; \n(c) the number of projects in Uttar Pradesh which are not \ncompleted in the s aid circuit and the time by which it is likely \nto be completed; \n(d) whether the Government is considering to include Hastinapur \nWildlife Sanctuary in consultation with State Government in \nthe said circuit of SDS; and  \n(e) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF TOURISM             (SHRI G. KISHAN REDDY) \n \n(a) to (e): A statement is laid on the table of the House. \n \n \n******** \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nSTATEMENT IN REPLY TO PARTS (a ) TO (e ) OF  LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.  *49 ANSWERED ON 06.02.2013  \nREGARDING DEVELOPMENT OF ECO-TOURISM CIRCUIT. \n(a) to (e): The Min istry of Tourism, Government of India launched \n‘Swadesh Darshan Scheme’ (SDS) in the year 2014 -15 for integrated \ndevelopment of Theme -Based Tourist Circuits in the country . Th is \nMinistry has sanctioned  a total of 76 projects under SDS for an \namount of Rs. 5315.59 Crore across the country under  identified \nthematic circuits including 8 projects in Uttar Pradesh, however no \nproject has been sanctioned under Eco -tourism circuit in the State \nof Uttar Pradesh. \n   \nThe details of the projects sanctioned under  eco-tourism \ncircuit of Swadesh Darshan Scheme in the country is as under: -   \n                                     \nS.No. Name of State Year of \nsanction \nProject Name Amt. \nSanctioned \n(Rs. in \ncrore) \n1. Uttarakhand (2015-16) Development of Tehri-\nChamba- Sarain aro und \nTehri Lake. \n69.17 \n2. Telangana (2015-16) Development of  \nMahaboobnagar \ndistricts (Somasila, \nSingotam, \nKadalaivanam, \nAkkamahadevi, \nEgalanpanta, \nFarahabad, Uma \nMaheshwaram, \nMallelatheertham) \n91.62 \n3. Kerala (2015-16) Development of \nPathanamthitta- Gavi- \nVagamon- Thekkady. \n64.08 \n4. Mizoram (2016-17) Development of  Aizawl -\nRawpuichhip - \n66.37 \n\nKhawhphawp - Lengpui \n– Durtlang -Chatlang- \nSakawrhmuituaitlang -\n Muthee – Beratlawng -\nTuirial Airfield – \nHmuifang \n5. Madhya \nPradesh \n(2017-18) Development of \nGandhisagar Dam - \nMandleshwar Dam - \nOmkareshwar Dam- Indira \nSagar Dam - Tawa Dam - \nBargi Dam - Bheda Ghat - \nBansagar Dam- Ken River \n93.92  \n6. Jharkhand (2018-19) Development of Dalma -  \nBetla National park - \nMirchaiya- Netarhat \n30.11 \n \n \nThe Swadesh Darshan scheme is n ow being implemented by \nthe Ministry of Tourism as Swadesh Darshan  2.0 (SD2.0) with the \nobjective to develop sustainable and responsible tourism \ndestinations following a destination and tourism -centric approach. \nThe SD2.0 Scheme guidelines envisage for sel ection of Destinations \nbased on State Perspective Plan (SPP) prepa red by the States. The \nSPP submitted by the State Government of Uttar Pradesh does not \ninclude development of Hastinapur Wildlife Sanctuary. However, as \nof now ‘Prayagraj’ has been identifie d as a destination for \ndevelopment under SD2.0 in the State of Uttar Pradesh. \n   \n******** \n \n \n \n \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S11_U_5": "\u0001\u0002\u0003\u0004\u0005\u0006\u0007\u0004\u0006\b\t\u0002\n\t\u000b\u0006\f\u000b\r\t\n\u0007\u000b\u0006\u000b\u000e\b\u0005\u000f\t\u0002\n\t\u0010\u0002\u0011\u000e\u000b\u0006\u0001\t\r\u0006\f\t\u0011\u0005\u0012\r\u0006\t\r\n\n\r\u000b\u0005\u000e\t\n\t\n\u0013\u0002\u0014\t\t\u000e\r\u0012\u0010\r\t\t\n\u000e\b\r\u0005\u0005\u0004\f\t\u0015\u0011\u0004\u000e\b\u000b\u0002\u0006\t\t\u0006\u0002\u0016\t\u0017\t\n\t\n\b\u0002\t\u0012\u0004\t\r\u0006\u000e\u0018\u0004\u0005\u0004\f\t\u0002\u0006\t\n\u0004\u0012\u0005\u0011\r\u0005\u000f\t\u0019\u001a\u001b\t\u001a\u0019\u001a\u001c\t\n\t\n\b\r\u0005\u0001\u0004\b\u000e\t\u0011\u0006\f\u0004\u0005\t\r\u0007\u0005\u0011\b\t\u000e\u001d\u0010\u0004\u0007\u0004\t\n\t\n\u0006\u0002\u0016\t\u0017\u0016\t\t\u000e\u0010\u0005\u000b\t\u0012\u0005\u000b\u001e\u0004\u0006\f\u0005\r\t\u000e\u000b\u0006\u0001\u0010\t\u001f\t\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\n\u0018 !!\t\"#$\t\u0007 % &\"$'\t()\t\u0010\u0002\u0011\u000e\u000b\u0006\u0001\t\r\u0006\f\t\u0011\u0005\u0012\r\u0006\t\r\n\n\r\u000b\u0005\u000e\t*$\t+ !$,&$-\t \n\"(\t&\",\"$\u001f\t\n.,/ \u0001 \"#$\t+'(0'$&&\t1,-$\t\"(2,'-&\t,3# $4 %0\t\"#$\t\",'0$\"\t()\t' $+!,3 %0\t \n&\"'$$\"\t ! 0#\"&\t 2 \"#\t \u0013\u0004\f&\t 5%-$'\t \r\",!\t \u0007 && (%\t )('\t \u0005$654$%,\" (%\t \n,%-\t\u0011'*,%\t\b',%&)('1,\" (%\t.\r\u0007\u0005\u0011\b/7\t\n.*/ \u0001 2#$\"#$'\t\"#$'$\t#,4$\t*$$%\t-$!,8&\t %\t,3# $4 %0\t\"#$\t&,1$7\t\n.3/ \u0001  )\t&(\u001b\t\"#$\t'$,&(%&\t\"#$'$)('7\t,%-\t\n.-/ \u0001 \"#$\t 95,%\"51\t ()\t )5%-&\t ,!!(3,\"$-\u001b\t '$!$,&$-\t ,%-\t 5\" ! :$-\t 5%-$'\t \n\"#$\t&, -\t&3#$1$\t-5' %0\t\"#$\t!,&\"\t\"#'$$\t) %,%3 ,!\t8$,'&;\t\n\t\n\r\u0006\u000e\u0018\u0004\u0005\t\n\u0001\n\b\u0010\u0004\t\u0007\u000b\u0006\u000b\u000e\b\u0004\u0005\t\u0002\n\t\u0010\u0002\u0011\u000e\u000b\u0006\u0001\t\r\u0006\f\t\u0011\u0005\u0012\r\u0006\t\r\n\n\r\u000b\u0005\u000e\t\n.\u000e\u0010\u0005\u000b\t\u0010\r\u0005\f\u0004\u0004<\t\u000e\u0