    "ls|LS17_S11_U_87": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 87 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 08.02.2023 \n \nACCIDENTS ON RAILWAY TRACKS \n \n*87. SHRI PRADYUT BORDOLOI: \n  SHRI FEROZE VARUN GANDHI: \n \n          Will the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state:   \n(a) the details of death of humans/ elephants/ animals that occurred on \nrailway tracks and were reported from 2014 till date, State/ zone/ \nyear-wise; \n(b) whether the Government has taken cognizance of high number of \nelephant deaths on railway tracks in the country, particularly in \nAssam during recent years; \n(c) if so, whether any general advisories have been issued to curb such \naccidental deaths; \n(d) whether inspections have been carried out to verify implementation \nof these advisories, if so, the details thereof along with the penalties \nimposed for non-compliance; \n(e) whether under-passes/ ramps track diversion/ enforcement of speed \nlimits have been undertaken to prevent such death of humans and \nanimals; and \n(f) if so, the details thereof along with other measures taken in this \nregard? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF RAILWAYS, COMMUNICATIONS AND \nELECTRONICS & INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY   \n(SHRI ASHWINI VAISHNAW) \n \n(a) to (f): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n***** \n \n\n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (f) OF STARRED \nQUESTION NO. 87 BY SHRI PRADYUT BORDOLOI AND SHRI FEROZE VARUN \nGANDHI TO BE ANSWERED IN LOK SABHA ON 08.02.2023 REGARDING \nACCIDENTS ON RAILWAY TRACKS \n \n(a): The State -wise number of deaths of hum an reported on railways as \nprovided in Accidental Deaths and Suicides in India (ADSI) data published by \nNational Crime Records Bureau (NCRB) for the last 8 years i.e. 2014 to 2021 \nare appended as Appendix-I.  Figures for the year 2022 and the current year \nhave not been published by NCRB. \nThe Zone -wise details of death of elephants on railway tracks during the \nlast 9 years i.e. 2014 to 2022 are appended as Appendix-II. \nThe desired information regarding number of animal deaths occurred on \nrailway tracks from 2014 to till date is quite wide in terms of its coverage \nand such a data is not maintained. \n(b) to (f): Yes , Sir.  A General Advisory was issued jointly by the Ministry of \nRailways and Ministry of Environment and Forests on 30 th March, 2010 to \nprevent train accidents involving Elephants. This advisory prescribes \ndetailed guidelines for Railways and Forest officials and measures to \nmitigate deaths of animals in train accidents.   \nFurther, Northeast Frontier Railway (N.F.Railway) has taken  the following \nsteps to mitigate the death of animals including elephants on Railway track \nby train hits, including the preventive measures mentioned in General \nAdvisories to prevent train accidents involving Elephant (Jointly issued by \nMinistry of Railway s & Ministry of Environment, Forests and Climate \nChange), which can be broadly classified in 4 categories: -  \n--2/- \n \n\n-2- \n1. Operational Measures: \n(i) Imposition of permanent speed restrictions at 86 numbers of \nelephant corridors in N.F.Railway. \n(ii) On receipt of information regarding movement of elephants, \ncaution orders are issued to Loco Pilots to pass cautiously and be \nprepared to stop due to elephant movement and in addition \nthereto temporary speed restrictions are imposed which varies \nfrom time to time as per  advice given by Forest department of \nrespective states. \n(iii) Deepor Beel is one of the major locations having large population \nof endangered Asiatic elephant.   Adequate Permanent Speed \nRestrictions have been imposed in the section of Azra – \nKamakhya, which passes through Deepor Beel.  \n2. Engineering Measures: \n(i) Clearing of vegetation along railway tracks in all the identified \nelephant corridors within Railway area. \n(ii) Provision of Signage boards at all the identified elephant corridors \nto pre-warn the loco pilots. \n(iii) Based on a joint survey by Railways and the Forest Department of \nGovernment of West Bengal, three out of four identified works \nwere sanctioned by Ministry of Environment and Forests to be \nexecuted by Railway on deposit term. The works executed by \nRailway are as under: \na) Construction of Passes – Passes with 20 meter width have \nbeen constructed at Km No. 66/2 -3 (LH), 67/3 -4 (LH), 67/8 -9 \n(Both side), 68/0 -1 (Both side) & 68/3 -4 (Both side) between \nChalsa and Nagrakata stations. \n--3/- \n\n-3- \nb) Construction of Ramps – Two number of earthen Ramps of 20 \nmeter width at Km No.  128/8-9 & 130/0 -2 and another one \nnumber of Ramp at Km No.  129/8-9 have been constructed \nbetween Madarihat and Hasimara stations. \nc) Construction of Girder Bridge and Fencing – A girder bridge \nhas been cons tructed at Km No. 24/6 -7 and rail fencing \nerected at Km. No. 25/9 -26/0 & 26/2 -3 between Gulma and \nSevok stations. \n3. Technological initiatives: \n(i) 75 number of Honey Bees sound systems have been propagated \nat manned level crossings, which act as a repellent to m ove away \nelephants from track. \n(ii) Provision of electric wire fencing at elephant corridor in Dudhnoi - \nKrishnai section, trench of 1.5 meter deep and 1  meter wide on \neither side of the track in Mariani -Nakachari section for 250 \nmeters and erection of rail fenc ing between Gulma and Sevok \nstations have been made. \n(iii) Innovative Intrusion Detection System to detect movement of \nelephant across Railway track has been introduced in Lumding – \nHawaipur section (24 Km) of Lumding Division in Assam \ncommissioned in August’ 22 and in Chalsa – Hasimara section (60 \nKm) of Alipurduar Division in West Bengal commissioned in \nDecember’ 22. \n4. Other measures: \n(i) Sensitization of all loco pilots , guards, station masters and forest \nofficials over N.F.Railway through awareness programme,  \n--4/- \n\n-4- \nworkshops etc.  \n(ii) Passengers and Pantry car staff are motivated/counseled through \nposters, Passenger Address system etc. not to throw any waste \nfood etc. on the railway track which may attract wild animals on \nrailway track. \n(iii) Further a permanent Co-ordination Committee has been \nconstituted between Ministry of Railways and Ministry of \nEnvironment, Forests and Climate Change for preventing elephant \ndeath in train accidents. \nImplementation of items of general advisory is checked during regular \ninspections by supervisors & officers and necessary remedial action is \ntaken for any shortfall. \n‘Police’ and ‘Public Order’ are State subjects under the Seventh Schedule to \nthe Constitution of India and, as such, State Governments are responsible \nfor prevention,  detection, registration and investigation of crime and \nmaintaining law and order etc. on Railways through their law enforcement \nagencies viz. Government Railway Police (GRP)/District Police. However, \nRailway Protection Force (RPF) supplements the efforts of GRP/District \nPolice to provide better protection and security of passenger area and \npassengers and for matters connected therewith. The State Police registers \ncases under Section 174 of Cr.PC in cases of unnatural deaths including \nunnatural deaths on ra ilways, conducts investigation and takes further \naction as per law.  \nThe following corrective measures are taken by the Railways to prevent \nhuman deaths in Untoward Incidents on Railway track:- \n(i) Railways have constituted interdepartmental ‘Joint Committee’  \n--5/- \n\n-5- \ncomprising officers of Safety, Security, Signal & Engineering \ndepartments across all Zonal Railways to study the causes and \nsuggest specific measures to minimise  deaths due to untoward \nincidents including trespassing by humans.  Accordingly, preventive \nand corrective measures are taken to improve and create \ninfrastructure to minimise casualties.  \n(ii) Regular announcements are made through Passenger Address System \nat Railway stations urging passengers to use foot over bridges (FOBs) \nand to avoid crossing of railway tracks.    \n(iii) Various awareness campaigns are organized by Railways to sensitize \npassengers about the fatalities of crossing railway tracks, foot -\nboard/roof-top travelling, boarding/de-boarding running trains etc.  \n(iv) Regular drives are conducted against trespassing, travelling on foot -\nboard, steps, roof top of trains, boarding/de-boarding running trains and \nthe persons apprehended are prosecuted under the relevant provisions \nof the Railways Act, 1989. \n(v) Railway Protection Force personnel are deployed on locations \nvulnerable for trespassing.  \n(vi) Erection of boundary wall/fencing at identified locations, vulnerable to \ntrespass.  \n(vii) Warning sign boards are provided at conspicu ous places for the \nawareness of passengers. \n(viii) Unauthorized trespassing on Railway premises including the Railway \ntrack is a punishable offence under section 147 of the Railways Act, \n1989. During the previous year i.e. 2022, total 124499 persons were \nprosecuted for trespassing on railways from whom an amount of ₹. \n3.92 Cr. have been realized as fine by Hon’ble courts. \n***** \n\nAPPENDIX-I REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) OF STARRED QUESTION \nNO. 87 BY SHRI PRADYUT BORDOLOI AND SHRI FEROZE VARUN GANDHI TO \nBE ANSWERED IN LOK SABHA ON 08.02.2023 REGARDING ACCIDENTS ON \nRAILWAY TRACKS \n(a): The State/UT -wise details of number of deaths of humans reported on \nrailways as provided in Accidental Deaths and Suicides in India(ADSI)data \npublished by NCRB for the years 2014 to 2021 are as under:- \nState/UT \nNumber of deaths of human reported on railways and \npublished in ADSI during the year 2014 to 2021 \n2014 2015 2016 2017 2018 2019 2020 2021 \nANDHRA PRADESH 1469 1401 1396 1365 1532 1519 613 760 \nARUNACHAL PRADESH 0 1 0 0 0 0 0 0 \nASSAM 690 674 636 571 579 598 422 461 \nBIHAR  1089 1907 1768 1763 1807 1864 972 1314 \nCHHATTISGARH 664 531 50